\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close Marginally Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Perez-Ferreiro   \par \uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2013\par 22:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Chile--Chile's IPSA select stock index ended marginally higher Wednesday, ignoring strong gains among U.S. and European shares, as analysts believe there was some profit-taking in the local market after Tuesday's 1.2% surge.\par \par \uc2 The IPSA closed 0.1% higher at 4353.6, while market volume increased to 114 billion Chilean pesos ($243 million) from CLP84 billion Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The local index made a strong move over the 4300-point level this week, rebounding from a five-session losing streak last week.\par \par \uc2 But Wednesday, the IPSA did not take its cues from the \uc2 Dow Jones Industrial\uc2  Average or the Stoxx Europe 600, which rose broadly with strong economic data from China and Europe, and minutes from the U.S. Federal Reserve that did not shake the confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's support for the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 As the U.S. and Europe are among the country's main trading partners, the local index usually takes direction from economic and political developments in those markets.\par \par \uc2 "The local market moved considerably higher yesterday, and today there might have been some profit taking, especially around shares that rebounded from last week's heavy losses," said German Guerrero, partner at MBI Inversiones.\par \par \uc2 Heavyweight retailer \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CNCO, CENCOSUD.SN) closed 0.2% lower to CLP2,780.8, after posting a 1.1% gain Tuesday, while Latam Airlines Group (LFL, LAN.SN) declined 0.5% to CLP9,886.6 after surging 2.5% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 However, another big winner from Tuesday, steel producer CAP (CAP.SN), rose 0.8% to CLP15,557.0\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, the peso closed at CLP468.50 to the dollar, down from Tuesday's 20-month high of CLP466.80, after what has been characterized as a "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " from \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister, who announced he would meet this week with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials to talk about the "worrisome" exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit 20-month highs this week and seemed headed to the CLP465 level where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2011, when it launched a $12 billion, year-long \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold its monetary policy meeting Thursday and may refer to the exchange rate in its policy statement issued immediately afterward.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCUs closed at 2.59%, up from 2.57% Tuesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs also closed higher, at 2.47%, from 2.46% the previous session. Bond yields move inversely to prices.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 Write to Sebastian Perez-Ferreiro at sebastian.perez-ferreiros@dowjones.com [ 10-04-13 2051GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130410e94a000u4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
