\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  rally on Fed stimulus hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2013\par 22:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Poor U.S. jobs data fuels bets on continued Fed stimulus\par \par \uc2 * Brazilian real jumps nearly 1.5 pct, crosses 2/dlr mark\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso jumps 1 pct to 19-month high\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso hits 1-1/2-year high; \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  eyed\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, April 5 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real jumped nearly 1.5 percent while the Mexican peso hit a 19-month high on Friday after weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data upheld expectations of continued monetary stimulus by the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 Other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also rallied as hopes for a prolonged bond-buying program by the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added to the support from Thursday's announcement of unprecedented monetary stimulus by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso closed at a more than 1-1/2-year high, nearing levels that, according to analysts, could trigger a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso initially weakened after data showed that hiring by employers in the United States, Mexico's main trading partner, hit a nine-month low in March.\par \par \uc2 Although the data raised doubts about the recovery of the world's largest economy, bets that the worrisome jobs outlook would keep the Fed's monetary stimulus in place eventually bolstered higher-yielding emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso reversed losses to jump 1 percent to 12.1849 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "U.S. payrolls were bad, fueling expectations that a loose monetary policy will remain in place in the United States," said a trader with a large Brazilian bank in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real strengthened past the psychologically relevant level of 2 per dollar for the first time in over two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The gains were also fueled by rumors that the government could ease a financial tax known as IOF over fixed-income investments by foreigners, traders said. A spokesman at \uc2 Brazil's Finance Ministry\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The real is unlikely to gain much further, however, as Brazilian policymakers seem likely to keep the exchange rate near the 2-per-dollar mark, considered by analysts as an appropriate level to avoid inflation pressures without hurting exporters.\par \par \uc2 Upholding that view, a senior official on President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 's economic team told Reuters that "the exchange rate is fine where it is."\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, policymakers looked increasingly likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains as the peso closed at 468.80 per dollar, increasing year-to-date gains to more than 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The trend for the peso is still positive. We see the market is shorting the dollar. The next level investors will test is 465 (per dollar), and then we can expect an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Matias Madrid, chief economist with Penta bank in Santiago.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2000 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                          Latest           \par Brazil real                1.9865     1.43     2.69\par                         