\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ \uc2 Chile  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Sees 2013 GDP Growing Between 4.5% And 5.5%\b0\par\par\uc2 978 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2013\par 16:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  \uc2 Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reduces '13 Inflation View, Sees Yr-End CPI At 2.8%\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  \uc2 Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Expects Domestic Demand To Grow 6.1% In 2013\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  Chile 2013 Current Account Deficit Seen Widening To 4.4% Of GDP-\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  \uc2 Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Increases 2013 Copper Price View To Avg $3.50/Lb\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  Euro Zone Still A Significant External Risk To Chilean Economy-\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  Strong Chile Domestic Demand Is Biggest Internal Risk -\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  Abrupt Copper Price Drop Would Impact Chile Current Account -\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  Chile Peso Appreciation Similar To Other Emerging Market \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  Chile Peso Still In Line With Fundamentals, Despite Strength-\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:16 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reiterates \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Can Be Costly\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:17 EDT *DJ  \uc2 Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's View Includes Stable Rate Outlook for Coming Months\par \par \uc2 2 Apr 2013 10:17 EDT DJ  \uc2 Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Raises 2013 GDP Outlook to 4.5%-5.5% Range\par \par \uc2 By Carolina Pica\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Chile--The resilient Chilean economy will likely grow at a brisk pace this year amid tame inflation and low unemployment, but it still faces risks from the euro zone, possible copper price decreases and dynamic domestic demand, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In its first Monetary Policy Report for the year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  upwardly revised its gross domestic product growth outlook to a range between 4.5% and 5.5% and slightly reduced its year-end inflation view to 2.8%, still very close to its 3% target.\par \par \uc2 It warned, however, the economy faces both external and internal risks and that these risks could have repercussions on the current account, which this year is expected to post a deficit of 4.4% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 "An abrupt change in external conditions, such as reduced terms of trade or less favorable financial conditions, could mean costly adjustments for the nation," bank Governor Rodrigo Vergara said in a speech to the Senate Finance Committee where he presented the report.\par \par \uc2 On the international front, the bank said that while the outlook for the U.S and China has improved, the euro zone faces several threats that could have local repercussions. The Chilean economy relies heavily on its exports, especially its copper exports, and China, the U.S. and Europe are the main destinations for Chilean goods.\par \par \uc2 For 2013, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased its copper price estimate to an average $3.50 a pound, from $3.40 in its previous report, and cautioned that a brusque decrease in international copper prices due to real or financial factors would have a significant effect on the current account, domestic demand and on inflation.\par \par \uc2 Still relatively high copper prices, robust domestic growth in the past three years, a wide spread between local and international interest rates and a weak dollar globally have all contributed to a firmer peso in relation to the dollar. In 2012, the peso appreciated around 8% versus the greenback and has gained another 1% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has firmed both against the dollar and against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, its gains are no different than those seen in other commodity-producing or emerging-market nations. It added that when measured in real terms the peso's exchange rate is on the stronger end of those values consistent with long-term fundamentals, but still within the bank's comfort zone. A stronger peso makes Chile's exports less competitive abroad.\par \par \uc2 In his prepared remarks, Mr. Vergara reiterated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the peso, like the bank did in 2011, can be costly.\par \par \uc2 As to the internal risks the local economy faces, the pace at which domestic demand continues to grow is also a concern for the bank, Mr. Vergara said.\par \par \uc2 Domestic demand this year is seen gaining 6.1% year-on-year on the back of sizable investments, especially in mining and energy projects, and also on robust private consumption.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vergara noted that data for the first quarter of this year indicate that strong retail, automobile, appliance and home furnishing sales point to still dynamic private consumption.\par \par \uc2 "The biggest risks are in relation to persistently elevated dynamism in domestic demand," Mr. Vergara said.\par \par \uc2 Strong domestic spending could, in the medium-term, put additional pressure on the labor market, the use of installed capacity and inflation, he said.\par \par \uc2 Regarding monetary policy, the bank noted the baseline interest rate scenario it used for this report fixed the benchmark overnight lending rate, or TPM, at its current level of 5% for the near term, in line with the most recent bank's polls of expectations.\par \par \uc2 In January 2012, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reduced the TPM 25 basis points to 5% and has held it there ever since, citing opposing factors such as the global economic slowdown, financial tensions from the euro zone's debt crisis and domestic demand that hasn't decelerated as quickly as anticipated.\par \par \uc2 "The TPM is at levels considered neutral, although its current level is high when compared with the rest of the world," Mr. Vergara said.\par \par \uc2 In the report, the bank noted that the spread with industrialized nations poses monetary policy challenges for many emerging-market nations, including Chile.\par \par \uc2 Write to Carolina Pica at carolina.pica@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 02, 2013 10:17 ET (14:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130402e942003on\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
