\par\par \b \uc2 Chile gov't says looking at ways to tame peso's gains -report\b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2013\par 14:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Feb 24 (Reuters) - Chile's government is studying measures to stem the peso's gains against the U.S. dollar such as selling more local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, the finance minister was quoted as telling a local newspaper on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The peso, which has been boosted by Chile's solid economic growth and healthy prices for top export copper, ranked among the strongest foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  performers against the U.S. dollar among 152 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by Reuters in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Felipe Larrain said last month that the government would support any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the strong peso.\par \par \uc2 In Sunday's interview with daily La Tercera, Larrain said the government was "studying some measures of macro-prudence due to the risks to the dollar."\par \par \uc2 Among possible steps, he mentioned establishing debt ceilings for certain state institutions, curbing public spending or issuing more peso-denominated debt.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso has firmed 1.12 percent against the dollar since the start of the year and gained 8.48 percent in 2012, making Chilean exports less competitive in global markets.\par \par \uc2 Larrain reiterated his opposition to capital controls to stem inflows from abroad.\par \par \uc2 "Under extreme circumstances, they might be considered but I'm skeptical about their effect. We will try not to take that path," he was quoted as saying.\par \par \uc2 He said the peso, like other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was feeling the effect of U.S. quantitative easing and expressed concern about the possible impact of Japan's decision to pursue economic stimulus measures.\par \par \uc2 "There's an enormous increase in the amount of money with an extremely low interest rate," he said.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 473.4 Chilean pesos)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Felipe Iturrieta; Writing by Helen Popper; Editing by Eric Beech)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-PESO/ | ABN | M | D | RNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130224e92o00066\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
