\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  lower; Mexico peso hit by rate cut fears\b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2013\par 20:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso falls 0.4 pct to lowest level in 2 weeks\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso drops with copper prices, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real closes flat due to Sao Paulo holiday\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Jan 25 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso led Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower on Friday as investors, fearing a possible interest rate cut by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pocketed some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent gains.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, the real closed flat at 2.0305 per dollar in an extremely thin market due to a holiday in Sao Paulo, the country's financial center.\par \par \uc2 Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses were exacerbated by the peso's fragile technical position on the Chicago exchange, where investors have placed large bets for a stronger Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts say such bets could quickly reverse, prompting the peso to sell off.\par \par \uc2 The peso dropped 0.4 percent to trade at 12.698 per dollar, after touching its weakest level in over two weeks.\par \par \uc2 It has lost around 1 percent since last Friday, when the \uc2 Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it could ease its monetary policy if inflation continues to cool and the economy loses steam. Lower rates could reduce the allure of peso-denominated assets.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's warning represented an abrupt reversal from its previous threat to raise interest rates, leaving some investors wondering whether policymakers were actually more concerned about the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's about face, coming as it did after a bout of peso strength, is likely to be viewed by many as a first sign of a possible policy response if the peso gains further ground," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's analyst \uc2 Luis Arcentales\uc2  wrote in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Latin America, the Chilean peso lost 0.3 percent to 471.80 per dollar, pressured by lower prices of copper, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top export.\par \par \uc2 Fears that Chilean policymakers may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength also weighed on the peso, which has gained about 1.5 percent since the beginning of the year.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1910 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                            Daily  YTD pct\par                                      pct   change\par                          Latest   change  \par Brazil real                2.0305     0.00     0.47\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.6980    -0.45     1.31\par                                           \par Chile peso               471.8000    -0.34     1.46\par                                           \par Colombia peso           1779.5000    -0.01    -0.76\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.5580    -0.12    -0.27\par                                           \par Argentina peso             4.9650    -0.05    -1.06\par (interbank)                                 \par                                           \pa