\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ \uc2 Chile  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Gov: Closely Monitoring Peso Strength Vs Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 999 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2013\par 17:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2013 11:08 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Can Be Costly - \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2   Chief Vergara\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2013 11:08 EDT *DJ  Capital Controls Don't Always Work - \uc2 Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's Vergara\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2013 11:08 EDT *DJ  Chile's Vergara: Increased Productivity Can Counter Effects of Strong Peso\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2013 11:08 EDT *DJ  Chile's Vergara: Investment Outflows Have Offset Capital Inflows\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2013 11:08 EDT *DJ  Chile's Vergara: Economic Balance 'Can Change at Any Moment'\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2013 11:08 EDT *DJ  Chile's Vergara Sees No Major Short-Term Threats to Inflation\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2013 11:08 EDT *DJ  Chile Market Understood Housing Bubble Warning, Says \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2013 11:08 EDT *DJ  Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2   Governor Vergara Spoke in Interview\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2013 11:28 EDT DJ  Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2   Vergara: Closely Monitoring Peso Strength Vs Dollar\par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  always monitoring peso market, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still within comfort range\par \par \uc2 --Increase in productivity can offset loss of competitiveness on stronger peso\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  satisfied that local market understands housing bubble warning\par \par \uc2 By Carolina Pica and Matthew Cowley\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Chile--The \uc2 Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping a close eye on the strength of the peso, although it remains in line with long-term fundamentals, its president said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Rodrigo Vergara said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at the stronger end of the bank's comfort range and, if it remains appreciated for long periods, it can damage the country's industrial base.\par \par \uc2 The peso strengthened 8% against the dollar in 2012, in part because of the monetary easing of developed economies such as the U.S and Europe to jump start growth. That hurts Chile's exporters, making their products more expensive, leading to fresh calls for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The institution \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2008 and 2011, and Mr. Vergara said it still has that tool. But he stressed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't the be-all and end-all for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  woes, adding there are always costs as well as benefits.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have a magic formula to control the exchange rate," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said in an interview. "Capital controls, for example, can distort a market and sometimes, they just don't work at all."\par \par \uc2 For Mr. Vergara, who travels later Wednesday to Davos, Switzerland, to participate in the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  , increasing productivity would be a better way to deal with the loss of competitiveness that comes from a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Structural reforms are really the way to deal with the strength of the peso, and we have seen some improvement in Chile's productivity in recent years," he said at his wood-paneled corner office at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's headquarters in downtown Santiago.\par \par \uc2 Chile's benchmark rate has stood at 5% for the last year, compared with the near-zero levels in industrialized economies. That spread, together with a positive economic growth outlook, has led to heavy capital flows into emerging markets. But Chile hasn't seen a significant increase in short-term speculative inflows.\par \par \uc2 "We've seen inflows but mostly aimed at [long-term] investments and this has been offset somewhat by outflows as Chilean companies and private pension fund managers invest abroad," Mr. Vergara said.\par \par \uc2 As in other emerging markets, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Chile\uc2  is having to walk a fine line on monetary policy, balancing a risky global outlook with a buoyant domestic economy. Economists say that any downturn in the global economy might prompt interest rate cuts, while signs that domestic demand is overheating could trigger interest rate increases.\par \par \uc2 The export-intensive Chilean economy grew faster than expected in 2012, at an estimated 5.5%--a figure that's especially eye-catching given that inflation ended the year at 1.5%, well below the bank's 3% target.\par \par \uc2 Robust domestic demand, amid low unemployment and rising salaries, was an important reason behind the growth surge last year, but, according to Mr. Vergara, investment also kept pace, keeping inflation pressures in check.\par \par \uc2 For 2013, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expecting growth to slow to around 4.75%, as demand for exports is decelerating. Chile is particularly dependent on copper, which accounts for 60% of exports and has seen prices come off from highs seen in 2011, although they remain close to record highs.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vergara said consumer demand nonetheless has been decelerating and should continue to slow in 2013. He said he doesn't see any major risks to prices in the short term and, gradually, inflation should return to the 3% target. On the other hand, the external outlook remains fragile. All that adds up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep rates on hold and to maintain a neutral bias, he said.\par \par \uc2 "This is a balance that can change at any moment," Mr. Vergara said.\par \par \uc2 That equilibrium is also reflected in market expectations. According to the bank's most recent monthly poll of more than 60 analysts, most expect a quarter-percentage-point increase in the benchmark rate by the end of the year to tame domestic demand. But one-fifth of the analysts see a rate cut by year-end, and another 20% see a half-point hike.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vergara mused that the split probably reflects whether people emphasize the internal or external factors. There aren't many periods in history when there's been such a contrast between two opposing economic forces, he added.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Mr. Vergara said he is "satisfied" that his warnings about the possibility of a housing bubble developing in some parts of the country had been heeded. "I'm satisfied that our message was heard," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Carolina Pica at carolina.pica@dowjones.com and to Matthew Cowley at matthew.cowley@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 23, 2013 11:28 ET (16:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130123e91n004f2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
