\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close Higher Tracking Gains in U.S. Shares\b0\par\par\uc2 By Graciela Ibanez   \par \uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2012\par 21:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Chile's IPSA select-stock index closed slightly higher Tuesday, tracking gains in U.S. shares on optimism about that country's budget negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The IPSA ended 0.2% higher, at 4288.38, while trading volume rose to 111.76 billion Chilean pesos ($235.7 million) from CLP82.32 billion Monday.\par \par \uc2 As several of the IPSA's heavyweight shares are also listed in the U.S., the Chilean select index often tracks the Dow Jones Industrial Average.\par \par \uc2 Among gainers, retail holding company \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CNCO, CENCOSUD.SN) ended 1.2% higher at CLP2,603. Meanwhile, department store Ripley (RIPLEY.SN) closed 2% higher at CLP465.29 after a local bank issued a recommendation to buy the stock.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  published its fourth and final Monetary Policy Report for the year. The bank revised its 2012 growth outlook upwards to 5.5%, while it expects Chilean gross domestic product to increase between 4.25% and 5.25% year-on-year in 2013.\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, the peso closed stronger against the dollar, also tracking higher U.S. shares.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP474.20 to the dollar, versus Monday's close of CLP474.70, after trading in a range of CLP474.20 to CLP475.60.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is an export-based economy, the peso often takes its cues from U.S. economic developments as they signal the direction of global growth.\par \par \uc2 The peso has strengthened about 8% against the dollar so far this year, prompting exporters to ask the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the peso. \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, didn't mention \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its Monetary Policy Report. A strong peso makes \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports less competitive.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched a yearlong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program when the peso firmed to the CLP465 level versus the dollar in early 2011.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed in a quiet session.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCUs ended at 2.55%, from 2.53% on Monday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.49%, same as on the previous session. Bond yields and prices move inversely.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 Write to Graciela Ibanez at graciela.ibanez@dowjones.com [ 18-12-12 2041GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121218e8ci000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
