\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen Farther Away\b0\par\par\uc2 1,432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2012\par 23:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 17:09 EST - Earlier this year in several post monetary policy meeting statements, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Chile\uc2  acknowleged the peso's strength against the dollar. In today's statement, it did the opposite, saying the dollar has gained against rivals on international markets. Analysts interpreted this nod to the dollar as a sign the bank won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the peso is still in line with long-term fundamentals. The peso has gained around 8% against the greenback this year. (carolina.pica@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 16:47 EST - With his sharp-tongued calls for corporate shakeups, billionaire investor \uc2 Carl Icahn\uc2  has surely inspired some boardroom groans over the years. Railcar maker Greenbrier (GBX) is playing nice, however, following Icahn's disclosure that he has taken a 10% stake in GBX and plans to discuss potential strategic options. CEO Bill Furman calls Icahn a "a respected investor" that GBX has had "considerable interaction" with in the past, chiefly by ways of Icahn's majority investment in peer American Railcar (ARII). Shares, which closed up 20% today at $16.73, were slightly higher after hours. (mia.lamar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 16:46 EST - Standoff with China and India, two huge potash consumers, is a growing headache for the fertilizer industry. Mosaic (MOS) lowers its volume guidance for current F2Q, saying the two countries' refusal to sign new potash purchase agreements is lowering price expectations, causing buyers in other countries to back off as well. Lowers potash volume to 1.3M-1.4M tonnes, down from an early October estimate of 1.6M-1.9M. Also narrows price guidance to upper end of previous range. But problems in potash are even spreading to the phosphate market, where buyers are also now delaying purchases; MOS lowers phosphate volume guidance as well. MOS down 4.9% at $48.26 after hours. (ian.berry@dowjones.com; @enberry)\par \par \uc2 16:46 EST - Top executive John Schappert's demotion and departure from \uc2 Zynga\uc2  (ZNGA) about three months ago is still shaking out. ZNGA said Tuesday it's promoting \uc2 David Ko\uc2 , \uc2 Barry Cottle\uc2  and \uc2 Steve Chiang\uc2  to top positions overseeing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , revenue and games, respectively. At one point in time all three of those aspects of ZNGA's business flowed through Schappert in one form or another. Schappert, a former \uc2 Electronic Arts (EA) \uc2 executive, had served as ZNGA's chief operating officer. He'd had some responsibilities taken away from him prior to his departure in early August. (john.letzing@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 16:26 EST - \uc2 Zynga\uc2  (ZNGA) CEO Mark Pincus told employees in an internal note published on a company blog today that \uc2 Dave Wehner\uc2 , the gaming firm's CFO now headed for a new gig with \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB), has been a "great partner for me." Wehner "remains a good friend to us all," Pincus wrote, adding that Wehner is being replaced by Mark Vranesh. Vranesh himself had been replaced as CFO by Wehner in 2010, and shifted to the role of chief accounting officer; Pincus notes Vranesh knows the role well. ZNGA up 1.4% after hours to $2.14. (john.letzing@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 16:11 EST - Expectation for a whippy session for US stocks plays out, with early declines quickly shaken off, rally ensues only to fade badly in the afternoon. \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 's strong advance after a beat-and-raise quarter helped erase an opening drop, and \uc2 IBM\uc2  shares built on HD's momentum to help muscle the Dow up as much as 83 points. But gains didn't hold as investors have trouble setting aside concerns about Europe's debt morass and looming US fiscal cliff. High-level executive departure at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  also added a weight on the Dow. DJIA falls 58.90 to 12756.18, near session lows, and Nasdaq Comp slides 20.37 to 2883.89. S&P 500 ends 5.50 lower at 1374.60. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 16:04 EST - Lorillard (LO) CEO \uc2 Murray Kessler\uc2  said the FDA's "radio silence" that has hindered the tobacco company's ability to bring new products to market this year has had "a slight tone change." Kessler said there has been some dialogue with the agency, which is required to sign off on new products beginning in March 2011. LO said the product delays are why the company hasn't been able to maintain 2011's strong growth, though the company is still posting sales and profit growth this year in an industry that has posted weaker volumes for years. LO ends up 1.7% at $114.51. (john.kell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 15:45 EST - In its open fighting with \uc2 IMF\uc2  on Greece, EU can find an ally in \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . The bank says Greece is not likely to meet its target of slashing the debt-to-GDP ratio to 120% by 2020. EU has signaled Greece should meet that target by 2022, but \uc2 IMF\uc2  wants Greece to stick to 2020. Goldman argues that a sizeable reduction in Greece's debt load is necessary to make Greek debt sustainable, but the bank says this option seems "politically unfeasible at the current juncture." Instead, Goldman says a more likely outcome will involve some debt relief for Greece, continued official-sector funding and a continued uncertain Greek debt profile. "This uncertainty will hold back a Greek recovery relative to a more decisive write-off and postpone a resolution of the Greek debt issues," the bank says. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 15:42 EST - An idea first advanced by Chicago Fed leader \uc2 Charles Evans\uc2  that monetary policy changes should be tied to achieving specific unemployment and inflation triggers is getting a big nod of support from Fed Vice Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  in a speech today. The dovish wing of central bankers has increasingly come to like the idea, although fellow travelers like the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  leader \uc2 William Dudley\uc2  are unsure whether such thresholds will be comprehensive enough to truly guide \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Still, the trigger concept increasingly appears to be an idea who's time has come. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 15:36 EST - The capital gains tax might make a stock's dividend seem less attractive to some investors, but \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  (MCD) says it'll continue a focus on dividends -- more so than share repurchases when it comes to returning cash to shareholders. MCD said it has spoken to some investors who've said they would prefer a focus on dividends even though there's still uncertainty around the related tax laws. MCD touted that its dividend has doubled since 2007 -- a silver lining to the gray cloud of its stock price, which has fallen 17% since hitting an all-time high in January. Shares near flat at $84.83. (annie.gasparro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 15:24 EST - A \uc2 Medtronic\uc2  (MDT) study casts doubt--surprise!--on a defibrillator part made by competitor \uc2 St. Jude Medical\uc2  (STJ). The article says the coating STJ uses to insulate its Durata wires, or leads, that connect defibrillators to patients' hearts may strain after being immersed in water. The study was published Tuesday in a journal of the \uc2 American Chemical Society\uc2 . "We do not rely on \uc2 Medtronic\uc2  to confirm the safety and reliability of material we use in our products," STJ said. Even considering the source, the study could place more fear, uncertainty and doubt on the integrity of STJ's leads in the minds of some doctors. STJ down 0.8% at $36.09. (joseph.walker@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 15:04 EST - Barron's speculated over the weekend that \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2 's \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway\uc2  (BRKA, BRKB) may have tried to acquire insurer Chubb (CB) earlier this year. Now \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analyst Jay Gelb, one of the panelists who asked questions of Buffett at this year's annual meeting, said CB is a good strategic and financial fit--and appears to meet the criteria Buffett laid out at that meeting. CB is a quality company with quality management and seems to be about the right size. Travelers (TRV) and Ace are too big, Gelb says, while XL and Arch Capital (ACGL) are too small. CB up 0.92% to $74.85. (erik.holm@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-13-12 1709ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121113e8bd000vn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
