\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Chile on Track to Grow 5% In 2012 - \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Governor\b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2012\par 16:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Effects of slower global growth on \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been softer than expected\par \par \uc2 --Peso \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not warranted, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor says\par \par \uc2 --Peso still in line with fundamentals, although on stronger end of comfort range\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details beginning in the sixth paragraph)\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Chile--The Chilean economy remains on track to grow around 5% this year, as the worsening global outlook hasn't hit the Andean nation as hard as expected, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Rodrigo Vergara said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Chile's gross domestic product, which relies heavily on exports, is expected to grow between 4.75% and 5.25% year-on-year in 2012, following gains of 6.1% and 6.0%, respectively, in 2010 and 2011.\par \par \uc2 Inflation, meanwhile, remains mild and is expected to end the year at 2.5%, below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target of 3%.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vergara warned that despite tame inflation, pressures are looming on the horizon, especially in light of a tight labor market, robust domestic demand and a closed output gap.\par \par \uc2 Demand this year is seen growing 5.6% year-on-year, while the unemployment rate is currently at 6.4%, a level which economist say puts the local economy at nearly full employment.\par \par \uc2 The good health of the Chilean economy, as well as a weak dollar globally and still high copper prices, have contributed to keeping the peso strong against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, the peso has gained nearly 10% against the dollar, prompting calls from agricultural exporters for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government measures to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vergara, who has previously said the bank could never rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  although it could be costly, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it hasn't been warranted.\par \par \uc2 "We reserve the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the situation merits it. If we haven't done so it's because we believe that this isn't the case for now," he said at a conference hosted by local charity Hogar Mision de Maria.\par \par \uc2 The peso's appreciation "is a source of concern for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mr. Vergara said, but he noted the peso's gains weren't isolated. "If we compared ourselves to Brazil, Colombia, Peru and Mexico, the peso's appreciation is in line with these other countries."\par \par \uc2 He reiterated that an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only have temporary effects and unlike in 2011, when the bank bought $50 million a day for a total $12 billion to weaken the peso, the Andean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still in line with its fundamentals, although on the stronger end of the bank's comfort range.\par \par \uc2 Write to Carolina Pica at carolina.pica@dowjones.com [ 25-10-12 1442GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121025e8ap0013y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
