\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Gov Hasn't Seen Cause to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2012\par 19:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:43 EDT - Although the Chilean peso is trading on the edge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comfort zone, the bank hasn't seen the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, its governor said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has firmed nearly 10% this year, fueling calls from exporters for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as government measures. Governor Rodrigo Vergara warned exporters that a weaker peso in relation to the dollar isn't the only solution to their competitiveness concerns as they should also strive to improve their productivity. (carolina.pica@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:20 EDT - Gold and the euro tend to move in step, but that link has been all but severed in recent days. The 30-day average correlation between gold futures and the euro was recently at -0.14, compared with a recent peak of +0.97 on Sep 26. Correlation is measured on a scale where +1.0 means the two assets move in the same direction, -1.0 means they move in opposite directions and 0 means there's no relationship. The sharp declines in correlation came as the euro stabilized while \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders watch for Spain to request a bailout, while gold prices fell as investors grew less anxious about the global economy and pared bets on higher gold prices. (tatyana.shumsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:19 EDT - The number of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  muni downgrades decreased in the 3Q compared to the 2Q, but the dollar amount of downgraded debt increased, pushing the YTD total of downgraded bonds above $200B. That dollar amount exceeds the $193.5B in debt downgraded in all of 2011, ratings agency says, and downgrades in 2012 have been 80% of all ratings actions year-to-date. Ratings actions on four issuers accounted for more than 70% of the debt downgraded in the 3Q: Port Authority of NY/NJ ($18.2B); Puerto Rico Sales Tax Financing Corp. ($16B), the state of Pennsylvania ($13.2B) and Chicago O'Hare airport ($6.5B). \uc2 Moody's\uc2  expects overall muni downgrade activity to continue to outweigh upgrades for the rest of 2012. (kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:19 EDT - With Mead Johnson (MJN) poised to close at its lowest level in 18 month, RBC says it wasn't just China and Hong Kong that was behind the baby-formula maker's miss-and-cut. The "shortfall vs. our estimate was evenly split between Asia/Latin America and North America/Europe." But the investment bank sees some "silver linings" in that the China/Hong Kong weakness "was more related to greater-than-expected distributor inventory reductions than a big consumption shortfall" while "management indicated that market share is sequentially improving." MJN is down 7.9% at $64. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:11 EDT - Relatively lackluster sale. The dark side is the bid-to-cover -- 2.56 is the lowest since at least March 2010. Indirect bid is around recent average -- 38.2% vs 40.4% for the past four sales. The notes are sold at 1.267%, the highest since April. The relief is strong direct bid, which at 18% is the highest since February. The bond market ticks down on the sale. The 10-year note is recently 14/32 lower in price to yield 1.824%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:10 EDT - Glowing ratings for \uc2 Comcast\uc2 's (CMCSA, CMCSK) NBC aren't limited to Nielsen this season--Wall Street is taking a kinder eye to the longtime underperforming network, too. ISI points optimistically to upcoming contract renewals that should boost retransmission fees and reverse compensation at the peacock network. As such, it upgrades its price target on majority owner CMCSA to $41 from $36 and notes the company's option to buy GE's stake in 2014 could also boost earnings. CMCSA reports 3Q results Friday. Shares are up 0.3% at $36.54 after having already surged 54% this year. (william.launder@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:07 EDT - Bombardier's (BBD.B.T) "biggest challenge" with its all-new CSeries jet is how it'll compete on price with rival aircraft makers when it's building "a handful of planes" vs high-volume line production from \uc2 Boeing\uc2  (BA) or Airbus, BA marketing executive Randy Tinseth tells reporters in Toronto. The more immediate concern for investors is if BBD.B.T hits its targets for first flight later this year and delivery next year. "There's nothing worse than disappointing your customers," says Tinseth, speaking from experience, given BA's repeated Dreamliner delays. "You just have to hope that the product is good enough that they forgive you." (caroline.vanhasselt@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:58 EDT - There's still a long way to go, but 3Q earnings may not be as bad as feared. With 49% of S&P 500 companies in, the rate of decline in year-over-year aggregate EPS is 1.3% as of today, versus down 2% for companies in as of Wednesday and down 2.5% on Tuesday, according to Thomson Reuters. On Oct. 1, EPS was seen declining 2.1% for the index's members. Also, the percentage of those companies beating EPS estimates has increased to 62% from Tuesday's 57%. Since 1994, 62% typically beat bottom-line forecasts. However, the big firms' top-line misses continue to mount. Just 36% of S&P 500 companies have exceeded revenue estimates; the rate was 42% a week ago. Those top-line shortfalls have been a key part of the recent stock-market weakness. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:51 EDT - Canada's aviation market has been growing faster than in the US because of low-cost carriers, such as WestJet (WJA.T) and Porter, \uc2 Randy Tinseth\uc2 , a marketing vice president at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  (BA) Commercial Airplanes tells reporters in Toronto, where BA unveiled its long-term North American forecast. Trend among LCCs is to fly "bigger and bigger" planes, he says, with the 50-seaters getting "pushed out" and replaced with around 70-seat turboprops, and the 70-90 seat category shifting into the 90-plus range. He expects LLCs worldwide to continue growing faster than network carriers during the next 20 years, but not at the pace seen during the past decade. (caroline.vanhasselt@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:50 EDT - Saying "not all great companies make for great investments all the time," Sterne Agee downgrades Crocs (CROX) to neutral after the footwear maker's miss-and-cut. "We do believe that CROX management has done a very-good job of resurrecting the brand from the abyss. However, we believe it is prudent to step away until we are comfortable that the company can consistently deliver on its guidance." Northland Capital notes Japan was the bugaboo to the 3Q. And several investment bank highlight the strength seen in CROX's bookings and average prices. Still, shares are down 19% at $13.04 after earlier hitting a 2-year low. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:49 EDT - Thursday reminds investors again that risky assets -- not Treasury bonds -- might be the punching bag if US credit rating gets axed. Last year, when S&P stripped the US of its triple-A, stocks sold off and Treasury bonds and the dollar rallied on safe-haven flows. Peter Tchir, founder of New York-based hedge fund TF Market Advisors, argues that even if Fitch or \uc2 Moody's\uc2  follow S&P to yank the US's top rating, Treasury yields are unlikely to jump. The Fed is still a buyer to support the economy and it won't tolerate much higher yields at this point, Tchir contends. A US downgrade would hit stocks and riskier segments of fixed-income markets, he says; in this case, S&P 500 stock index, recently down 0.1% at 1407.32, could drop to 1,375. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:44 EDT - It's safe to buy dollar-yen, but not necessarily at the current level of Y80.05, says Richard Franulovich at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . Buy it at Y79.60, which has been the support level for the past few days, he says. "That's a good spot to place a buy order," he said, adding "sell at anything above Y81." Any weakness in dollar-yen will be limited, he says "because it looks like an uptrend that would last until the BoJ meeting next week. Then, it depends on what they do." USD/JPY at 80.15, up 0.43%, according to EBS via CQG. (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-25-12 1343ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121025e8ap000qr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
