\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  cbank says not the moment for  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2012\par 14:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Chilean peso has strengthened nearly 8 pct this year\par \par \uc2 * Cbank says reserves itself right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  cbank president says peso strength a worry\par \par \uc2 (Recasts with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president quote, context)\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Oct 25 (Reuters) - The Chilean peso's strength remains a worry but it isn't the moment to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem its appreciation, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Rodrigo Vergara said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The peso has strengthened nearly 8 percent this year, sparking market speculation over a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Yes, it's a worry. We reserve ourselves the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the situation deserves it, but if we haven't done it yet it's because we don't think it's the moment at least for now," Vergara told a business forum.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deployed a dollar-purchasing program last year, which lasted through December, to curb peso strength after it appreciated to its highest level in more than 2-1/2 years at 465.50 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The peso was trading around 0.52 percent stronger at 478.50 per dollar on Thursday morning, buoyed by encouraging data from Britain and China and the U.S. Federal Reserve's latest commitment to support growth. Vergara's comments didn't appear to affect its movement.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso has firmed as an attractive interest rate differential compared with the developed world and high levels of foreign investment have kept dollars flowing into Chile, which is seen growing around 5 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark interest rate is 5 percent, compared with a U.S. overnight rate that is less than 0.25 percent, a gap that attracts capital to the South American nation.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting By Felipe Iturrieta. Writing by Alexandra Ulmer Editing by W Simon)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-CBANK/EXCHANGERATE (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | C | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121025e8ap0015k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
