\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mixed;  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  peso drops on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears\b0\par\par\uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2012\par 20:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  cenbank president doesn't rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Concerns over Spain ratings, banks weigh on Latam \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso falls 0.2 pct, Brazil real up 0.2 pct\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Sept 28 (Reuters) - Chile's peso dropped on Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains, while concerns about Spain's banks and credit ratings weighed on Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in general.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso lost 0.8 percent to change hands at 474.60 per U.S. dollar after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Rodrigo Vergara said policymakers may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during "exceptional periods" in which the exchange rate is significantly out of line with fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 The peso has gained more than 9 percent so far this year, which makes it the second strongest performer among the 152 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso lost a modest 0.2 percent to trade at 12.8577 per dollar as concerns about Spain made investors cautious about taking risks in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 A stress test on Spanish banks showed the institutions need a total of 59.3 billion euros in extra capital, in line with market expectations, but concerns about a possible downgrade of Spain's sovereign ratings by Moody's Investors Service still weighed on investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real decoupled from the rest of the market, rising 0.2 percent to 2.0260 per dollar, as investors tried to influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's closing rate at the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 "Markets are looking overseas, especially to the United States and Spain," said Ovidio Soares, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Interbolsa do Brasil, a brokerage in Sao Paulo. "But today the key issue is (the fight) between investors who are short or long in dollars."\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1840 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                            Daily  YTD pct\par                                      pct   change\par                          Latest   change  \par Brazil real                2.0260     0.25    -7.77\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.8577    -0.24     8.65\par                                           \par Argentina peso*            6.2800     0.32   -24.68\par                                           \par Chile peso               474.6000    -0.76     9.42\par                                           \par Colombia peso          1,800.3000    -0.10     7.67\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.5980    -0.08     3.81\par                                           \par * Argentine peso's rate between                    \par brokerages                                  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Natalia Cacioli in Sao Paulo, Moises Avila in Santiago; editing by Chizu Nomiyama, Gary Crosse)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | M | RBN | NAT | C | E | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD