\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Chile Fruit Exporters Anxious Over Peso's Gains; Worry About Industry\b0\par\par\uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2012\par 21:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Fruit-export trade group Fedefruta reiterates need for \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Producers and exporters still not familiar with hedging instruments\par \par \uc2 --Fedefruta asks for government help while the Chilean peso remains strong \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Graciela Ibanez \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SANTIAGO, Chile--The Chilean peso's 9.2% surge against the U.S. dollar this year is making Chile's fruit producers and exporters anxious as some 70% of their $4 billion annual sales are denominated in dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --on the back of the country's solid economic growth--is lifting the fruit-production sector's costs, which are largely denominated in pesos, to some 70% to 100% of revenue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fresh fruit accounted for 5% of Chile's 2011 exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While government officials have cheered Chile's economic performance in times of global uncertainty, the fruit-export trade group Fedefruta isn't happy and has called on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the peso.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With a strong peso, no one will want to invest in [agricultural] production," said Fedefruta President Cristian Allendes in an interview Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last year, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Chile\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market after the peso had firmed to a 32-month high at CLP465.40 to the dollar in the first days of January. The bank bought a total of $12 billion at a rate of $50 million a day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This year, the peso hasn't crossed the CLP470 psychological level--it is currently trading at around CLP474 to the dollar--and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gov. Rodrigo Vergara said last week it would be expensive for the bank to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among fruit producers, kiwi and plum producers are the most concerned with the current peso level as their costs are nearly matching their revenue, Mr. Allendes said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since the mid-2000s, exporters have had to live with a stronger peso. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedges became more common in the industry, but only large exporters and producers are familiar with them despite efforts from Fedefruta to promote these instruments across the industry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's a fragmented industry where around 80% of fruit producers are small- and medium-size, and they don't know how the hedging system works," said Mr. Allendes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When the peso weakened to nearly CLP520 to the dollar in late May and early June, producers and exporters took forward contracts for only $200 million, or 5% of the industry's sales, Mr. Allendes said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most of the sector's sales are denominated in dollars, but 30% of revenue is in euros, which exporters also convert to dollars. Fedefruta is trying to promote both dollar and euro forward contracts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Allendes recently met with other non-mining export trade groups to coordinate a public-opinion campaign. Non-mining exporters want Chileans to become aware of how the strong peso is eating into their competitiveness abroad.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If investments in production fall, the Chilean regions that depend on agriculture and fish farms would see jobs tumble," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The mining industry, which represents 60% of exports and 15% of gross domestic product, meanwhile, have a natural hedge against the strong peso as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  usually moves in line with prices of their products, especially copper.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Allendes also wants to see help from the government while the peso remains strong versus the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The government could give us more money to promote our exports abroad, so an increase in sales could partially offset a stronger peso," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government contributes around $10 million a year to the agriculture-exporting sector for advertising campaigns abroad, and Mr. Allendes would like to see that figure grow to at least $50 million for each of the next three years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government could also invest more in infrastructure projects such as roads in the agricultural-intensive regions, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Sebastian Pinera, meanwhile, earlier this week said exporters will have to learn to cope with the strong peso.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We will have to live with the exchange rate very similar to what we have now. For some sectors, it is a problem, and they will have to adjust," Mr. Pinera said during a visit to Australia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Graciela Ibanez at graciela.ibanez@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 11, 2012 15:29 ET (19:29 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120911e89b005p1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
