\par\par \b \uc2 Fed Easing Could Boost Some  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 , but Not All\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Hong   \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2012\par 19:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Last time the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  bought bonds, the dollar dropped between 5% and 10% against most of the world's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If the Fed goes ahead with more easing later this year, the greenback's decline is likely to be more selective, investors and analysts say.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Fed Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would provide additional support for the economy "as needed," in a speech that appeared to lay out the case for a third round of bond buying known as quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 As those measures would involve printing more dollars and pushing down yields on U.S. debt, investors are looking at other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold.\par \par \uc2 Their options are more limited than they were two years ago, the last time the Fed pursued easing. Central banks from Brazil to Switzerland have clamped down on trading in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market or cutting interest rates, all in a bid to curb volatility and shield exporters.\par \par \uc2 As a result, investors have increasingly turned to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Chilean peso, Mexican peso and South Korean won, where central banks have lately adopted a lighter touch.\par \par \uc2 "It's been a global race to the bottom between [actions by] a lot of central banks, and it doesn't look like it's going to ease off," said Bob Gelfond, chief executive officer of New York-based hedge fund MQS Asset Management, which holds G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the Chilean and Mexican pesos should appreciate on another round of Fed easing, given expectations that those central banks will hold rates steady in the coming months. The Chilean and Mexican pesos have outperformed other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last three months, appreciating over 7% against the dollar since June 1.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the Brazilian real has weakened 0.5% in the same period and the Colombian peso is nearly unchanged. \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut interest rates nine times over the past year, including another cut Wednesday, and has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to devalue the real and help local exporters.\par \par \uc2 "If it weren't for bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Brazilian real could rally another 5% or 6%, but with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sitting there," investors are more reluctant to buy the real, said Michael Mata, portfolio manager of the $700 million ING Global Bond Fund. Mr. Mata said he plans to buy the Mexican peso if the Fed launches another round of easing.\par \par \uc2 Another winner is the South Korean won, which was the top Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gainer in the past three months amid looming \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in Indonesia, Japan and India. After a surprise rate cut in July, \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held rates steady in early August.\par \par \uc2 However, even the more hands-off central banks have a breaking point. A Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official warned on Thursday that policy makers won't tolerate continued strength in the krone, which rose to a 16-week high against the dollar this week.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year, launching daily dollar purchases to stem its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. It could do so again if the peso strengthens to 470 to the dollar, analysts say. Friday, the peso was at 480.10 versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 But the market still sees a greater risk of the Fed easing than of some other central banks becoming more aggressive. For example, some of the Australian dollar's gains were eroded recently by signals from policy makers that the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is weighing on the economy. But the Aussie is still up 6.3% against the U.S. dollar since June 1, and the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  looks unlikely to cut interest rates soon.\par \par \uc2 --Erin McCarthy contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Nicole Hong at nicole.hong@dowjones.com.\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 08-31-12 1323ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020120831e88v0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
