\par\par \b \uc2 Chile peso on rise, could break 470/dlr with US stimulus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Moises Avila   \par \uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2012\par 00:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Aug 29 (Reuters) - Chile's peso will probably continue to appreciate in line with the Andean nation's strong economic fundamentals during the rest of the year and may even break technical resistance at the 470-per-dollar level if the United States unleashes a new round of monetary stimulus, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The peso has gained around 8 percent versus the dollar since the start of the year to trade near 480 per dollar, a level Finance Minister Felipe Larrain said has the government's attention.\par \par \uc2 Analysts see the peso trading in a range of 475 to 485 per dollar over the coming weeks, short of levels which have triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, as the market awaits clear signs of extra stimulus in the United States and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Markets are awaiting a speech by U.S. Federal Reserve chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  in Jackson Hole, Wyoming on Friday for hints on a third round of stimulus, which likely would translate into dollar inflows to emerging economies, and push up local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There's a strong psychological level at 473-470 (pesos per dollar) because there's more of a chance the \uc2 Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Sergio Tricio, head of research at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "But if quantitative measures in the United States are used, the peso could break that level," he said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used a dollar-purchasing program last year, which lasted through December, to beat back the peso after it appreciated to its highest level in more than 2-1/2 years at 465.50 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its key interest rate steady at 5.0 percent for a seventh straight month, saying energy and food prices could pick up in coming months, and mentioned the appreciation in the peso in what was viewed as a signal to the market.\par \par \uc2 "It's not just the key interest rate's level, but it's the neutral stance regarding rates that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has maintained up until now that's helped support the peso," Jorge Selaive, chief economist at bank BCI in Santiago said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The economy of Chile, the world's largest copper producer grew 5.5 percent in the second quarter, year-on-year, bolstered by mining, retail, forestry, agriculture and brisk domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 The peso has firmed as an attractive interest rate differential compared with the developed world and high levels of foreign investment have kept dollars flowing into Chile.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has some room to appreciate before any concrete \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures. Conversely, if expectations for stimulus are dashed and risk aversion returns, the peso could abruptly weaken to 500 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "It's likely we'd see some action on the part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or government if the peso breached 470 (per dollar). It's a complicated level when we have falling prices for our exports and sliding export volumes ... the sector's competitiveness is already facing a very complicated scenario," BCI's Selaive said.\par \par \uc2 Chilean exporters have been asking the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take action to stem the peso's strength to help make prices for their products more competitive.\par \par \uc2 The peso's current strength is attuned to Chile's economic reality, the former president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jose De Gregorio, told Reuters last week.\par \par \uc2 "The peso is within a range that is consistent with \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic fundamentals ... we said it when we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2011. The tension of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will last for a very simple reason: Chile is a strong economy and has extremely favorable conditions," De Gregorio said.\par \par \uc2 In 2008, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the peso firmed to 431 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Moises Avila, additional reporting by Anthony Esposito; writing by Anthony Esposito, editing by Carol Bishopric)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-PESO/OUTLOOK | ABN | D | M | RBN | C | E | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | ABX | BNX | FUN | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120829e88t001ar\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
