\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  peso surges on economy, but space before  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Moises Avila   \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2012\par 21:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Aug 10 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso, currently trading near 11-month highs, is likely to keep appreciating on the back of strong economic growth, but has room to gain another 3 percent against the dollar before any eventual \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders and analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The small, export-dependent economy of world No.1 copper producer Chile is bracing for fallout from the euro zone crisis and a slowdown in global demand, especially from top trade partner and metals consumer China.\par \par \uc2 But it expanded 5.5 percent during the first half of the year, one of the world's fastest rates, and this growth combined with interest rates held steady at 5 percent in recent months has lured foreign capital, in turn boosting the local peso.\par \par \uc2 "There are no external factors which justify such a strong increase in the peso. So the only explanation can be the local economy, which makes the country attractive to foreign capital," said Sergio Tricio, head of research at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chile brokerage.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated 9.37 percent against the dollar year-to-date. It grazed a September 2011 peak at 474.80/dlr on Thursday before easing slightly, though traders and analysts are betting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely only step in around the 460 level.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year when the peso rose to 465.50 per dollar, and made its move in 2008 when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to 430 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 But the bank says it has no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level trigger and focuses on whether the real exchange rate, which shows how competitive the peso is against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is in line with fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 "With the calculations that we have now done, a real exchange rate that is out of balance would be equivalent to 460 pesos. I don't think anything would happen before that level," said Matias Madrid, chief economist at Banco Penta in Santiago.\par \par \uc2 "Chile is not a safe-haven, but the good performance of the economy, a copper price which hasn't fallen as much (as during the 2008 global crisis), as well as the interest rate differential, given the rate is no longer seen falling, make it attractive," he added.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Rodrigo Vergara on Tuesday said he expected trade in the peso to remain volatile in coming months, citing prices for main export copper and international financial turmoil.\par \par \uc2 But he added that exchange rate flexibility remained the best tool to face volatile external conditions, and many in the market think the bank would opt for verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before taking any measures such as dollar purchases.\par \par \uc2 "It doesn't mean we flatly rule out the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when we consider the exchange rate to be out of alignment with fundamentals," he told a business forum on Tuesday. "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly in both 2008 and in 2011."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Tricio thinks the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could mention the peso in next week's interest rates decision statement, if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciates to the 470 level, as a signal to markets that the bank can always \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary.\par \par \uc2 Foreign capital inflows boost the supply of dollars available in the country, which are then cashed into pesos, boosting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes exports less competitive.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors who bought dollar forwards from local banks, are allowing the contracts to expire, betting the peso will continue to appreciate, meaning short-term capital is remaining in the country.\par \par \uc2 Once those contracts expire, the local banks who covered the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying dollars locally sell them, which injects U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the local market, boosting the peso.\par \par \uc2 Foreign bets against the peso in the futures market have fallen to $7.645 billion, down from a record near $10 billion three months ago, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data through Aug. 8.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Simon Gardner & Theodore d'Afflisio)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-PESO/ | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | C | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120810e88a0016w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
