\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Not Planning New  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,238 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2011\par 16:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:26 (Dow Jones) \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "fully satisfied" with its $12B \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year and isn't planning any more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says new bank Gov. Rodrigo Vergara. In his debut in that post before the local Icare business forum, Vergara emphatically ruled out any new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "as we don't see the need given current circumstances." (carolina.pica@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:23 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says \uc2 Nike\uc2 's (NKE) earnings outperformance this year has come not just from better top-line performance "but also due to continued reductions in marketing spending as a percentage of sales. We view (marketing) as a crucial investment for the company, and the price may be paid in future periods for what could prove to be underinvestment during a period of strong demand." The F2Q's rate of 11.2% was a 6-year low for the period. NKE is up 2% today at $95.48. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:19 (Dow Jones) It now looks like the housing bust was even worse than we thought. The National Association of Realtors, which took far to long to acknowledge the housing bust in the first place, now says sales were likely some 14% below previous estimates between 2007 and 2010. Shares of home builders fall after the report: Hovnanain (HOV) down 6.6% at $1.40, while Beazer (BZH) declines 0.5% to $2.43. (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:18 (Dow Jones) Research In Motion (RIMM) shares jump 7.5% to $13.46 on reports that the BlackBerry-maker held talks with several competitors about possible takeovers or mergers, but not everyone is buying. National Bank analysts in a note this morning say floor for RIMM shares has yet to be found. "We'd be selling into rallies led by M&A speculation. We continue to expect the stock will trade into the single digits as the company's fundamentals worsen," firm says. Look for "the vultures to circle and to wait for a lower entry point; if M&A bids surface." Keeps rating at underperform. (william.connors@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:16 (Dow Jones) A flattening of swap curves in EMEA markets reflect how little front room those economies have to cut rates since inflation is not decelerating while growth does, says Guillaume Tresca of \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2 . They also signal gradual economic slowdown in countries such as South Africa, Turkey, Poland and the Czech Republic, he adds, while a recession looks to be on its way to Hungary. In comparison, Asian and Latin American swap curves are steepening since these countries' central banks have more capacity to cut rates to fuel growth. (erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:16 (Dow Jones) The US is making some small progress paring down oversupply of housing. The Realtors say the inventory of existing homes listed for sale fell 5.8% in November to 2.58 million. That represented a seven-month supply at the current sales pace, compared a healthy level of about six months. Earlier today, \uc2 CoreLogic\uc2  said the October "shadow inventory" of homes (distressed, delinquent or foreclosed homes not yet on the market) stood at 1.6 million, a supply of 5 months. This was down from October 2010, when shadow inventory stood at 1.9 million units, or 7-months' supply. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:10 (Dow Jones) Realtors discover about 14% fewer existing homes were sold from 2007 through 2010. The Realtors' new figures show 2008 was the worst year for resales during the housing bust, with only 4.11 million sold, down from the previous estimate of 4.91 million. The downward sales number follows other revisions showing the last recession was the worst since Great Depression. In August, the government relooked at its data and said real GDP from 4Q 2007 to 2Q 2009 fell a cumulative 5.1%, compared with 4.1% in previously published estimate. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:10 (Dow Jones) Treasury are just a hair weaker after the release of the November existing-home-sales data and nearly 5 years of revisions, with 10-year notes bobbing into negative territory. They're down 1/32, yielding 1.929%, while the 30-year bond is leading declines, losing 15/32 to yield 2.951%. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:06 (Dow Jones) November sales of existing homes rise 4.0%, better than 2.0% expected. But revisions to past data show only 4.42 million hold sold last month, down sharply from the 5.07 million forecasted. Sales for all of 2011 are on track to hit around 4.25 million, up slightly from last year's level of 4.19 million, which was revised downward 15%, says National Association of Realtors. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:06 (Dow Jones) The initial euphoria about \uc2 KB Home\uc2 's (KBH) F4Q results has faded as the focus turns from better-than-expected EPS and revenue to a sharp drop in margins. Homebuilding gross margin slid to 14.7% from 19.1% a year earlier on fewer deliveries at higher-margin communities. Meanwhile, Ticonderoga says, "We are now worried that the big orders results of the last two quarters are not being made at a strong-enough margin, which would prevent useful profitability in 2012." While the sharp order gains, including 4Q's 38%, "will absolutely help the overall profitability...a much stronger gross margin than the current level must appear." KBH is down 5.7% at $7.30, wiping out most of Tuesday's 10% jump. (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:03 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Miller Tabak\uc2  chief technical market analyst \uc2 Phil Roth\uc2  says the normal year-end scenario for stocks calls for shallow consolidations this week, and a drift up on very light volume next week. He sees initial support at Monday's lows around 11735-11850 for DJIA and 1202-1215 for S&P 500. Meanwhile, resistance is in the area of the early December highs, around 12150-12250 for DJIA and 1250-1267 for S&P 500. "The real test for the medium-term outlook will come in January," Roth says. "Good years usually start with strong breadth and a big pick up in volume." DJIA down 53 at 12050, S&P 500 down 7 at 1234. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:01 (Dow Jones) BMO likes Smart Balance's (SMBL) health focus, but doesn't see much more room for shares to run. Firm starts SMBL at market perform, as "underlying earnings growth likely will not materially exceed that of its small/mid-cap peers." But there's still a lot of love. In addition to SMBL's healthy credentials, BMO is bullish on the spread maker's enhanced milk business, a possible "game changer" as long as it can scale it nationally. Firm also thinks SMBL's improved operating margins are sustainable. But SMBL's rich at 19-times earnings, and is above firm's $5.50 price target. SMBL down 1.7% to $5.59. (paul.ziobro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-21-11 1026ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111221e7cl000ab\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr